<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<!DOCTYPE plist PUBLIC "-//Apple//DTD PLIST 1.0//EN" "http://www.apple.com/DTDs/PropertyList-1.0.dtd">
<!-- Entitlements for NeoDropFinder.appex: the App Sandbox, plus an outgoing loopback connection.

     The App Sandbox is not a choice here. PlugInKit refuses to register an extension without it — the
     refusal is a line in the system log ("plug-ins must be sandboxed") and nothing else: `pluginkit -m
     -p com.apple.FinderSync` simply never lists it, and Finder shows no badges and no error.

     Two channels carry the badge index out of that sandbox:

       * The app writes it into the extension's container from outside. macOS 14+ protects a container
         from other teams, so this works only when both bundles carry the same team identity: an
         ad-hoc build (no team at all) is refused, and that channel stays dark.
       * The app serves the same bytes on loopback (a small fixed port range) and the extension fetches
         them over HTTP. That is what `network.client` is for, and it is the channel an ad-hoc build
         uses. Apple DTS confirms local-network privacy does not apply to loopback.

     A Mach service was tried first and does not work: `NSXPCListener(machServiceName:)` needs launchd
     to own the name, and a name registered by an ordinary app is invisible to a lookup — measured as
     "Connection init failed at lookup with error 3 - No such process" from a test process and from a
     real app started by LaunchServices.

     Deliberately absent: any file access, and any App Group. The extension never opens the user's
     files — Finder hands it URLs and the index hands it badge identifiers, and both are just strings
     — so it needs no user-selected, home-relative or network-server entitlement. An App Group cannot
     be the channel either: `~/Library/Group Containers` is closed to the non-sandboxed app (it must
     not be sandboxed, because it mirrors to /Volumes), and a sandboxed process is refused when
     another bundle created the container. scripts/verify.sh checks the sandbox, the absence of an App
     Group, and that this outgoing connection is declared. -->
<plist version="1.0">
<dict>
  <key>com.apple.security.app-sandbox</key>
  <true/>
  <key>com.apple.security.network.client</key>
  <true/>
</dict>
</plist>
